---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ForecastChart from '../components/ForecastChart.astro';
import { ArrowUpRight, GitCompareArrows, CalendarDays, Info } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Layout title="ENSO Forecast & Probability Changes" active="forecast"><div class="page-shell subpage"><div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">SEASONAL PROBABILITIES</span><h1>The outlook, <span>in perspective.</span></h1><p>Follow the odds. Compare the same season across different issues.</p></div><span class="demo-label"><Info size={14} /> Illustrative data</span></div><div class="forecast-summary"><div><span class="eyebrow">EXAMPLE DJF OUTLOOK</span><div class="forecast-big">78<span>%</span></div><p>El Niño probability · December–February 2026–27</p></div><div class="forecast-revision"><GitCompareArrows size={23} /><div><strong>+10 percentage points</strong><p>From 68% in the September example to 78% in October.<br />Both estimates refer to the same DJF season.</p></div></div></div><ForecastChart expanded /><section class="explanation-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">READING THE CHART</span><h2>Three outcomes. One season.</h2><p>Each bar represents a three-month season. The three probabilities add up to 100%. OND means October–December; DJF means December–February.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">COMPARING ISSUES</span><h2>Keep the target season fixed.</h2><p>A change from DJF to JFM is a change in forecast period. To understand a revision, compare predictions for DJF issued in two different months.</p></div></section><div class="source-callout"><CalendarDays size={20} /><div><strong>Official probabilities live at NOAA / CPC.</strong><p>These example issues demonstrate the comparison. They are not archived official forecasts.</p></div><a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/outlook/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open CPC outlook <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a></div></div><script>import { initCharts } from '../scripts/charts'; initCharts();</script></Layout>
